(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-metabolism-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Quiz 1 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The liver's contribution to fat digestion is its production of...","choices":["bile, which emulsifies fat to aid its digestion and absorption","hydrochloric acid, which breaks fat into fatty acids","lipase, the main enzyme that digests dietary fat","insulin, which drives fat storage after a meal as a general rule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The liver continuously produces bile, whose bile salts emulsify dietary fat into small droplets; this greatly increases the surface area for pancreatic lipase to act and helps form the micelles that carry fat products to the absorptive surface. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-17"},{"q":"During fasting, the liver supports other tissues by...","choices":["converting circulating ketones back into stored dietary fat","producing ketone bodies from fatty acids for use as fuel","storing all fatty acids so they cannot be used","releasing oxygen bound to stored hemoglobin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In fasting, the liver oxidizes fatty acids and converts the surplus acetyl-CoA into ketone bodies, which it exports as an alternative fuel for tissues such as the brain, sparing glucose during prolonged energy shortage. — Ch 69, slides 15-19; Ch 71, slide 12"},{"q":"The liver disposes of the nitrogen from amino-acid breakdown by...","choices":["converting toxic ammonia into urea for excretion by the kidneys","storing the ammonia in hepatocytes until it is needed","exhaling the ammonia directly through the lungs","converting the ammonia into glucose for energy under these conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Deamination of amino acids releases ammonia, which is highly toxic; the liver's urea cycle converts it into urea, a far less toxic compound that the kidneys excrete. Liver failure allows ammonia to accumulate. — Ch 70, slides 16-17; Ch 71, slide 10"},{"q":"The liver's role as a blood-glucose buffer means it...","choices":["converts all absorbed glucose immediately into triglyceride for storage","secretes insulin whenever plasma glucose rises above the normal range","prevents any glucose from reaching the systemic circulation directly","stores glucose as glycogen after meals and releases glucose between meals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the liver takes up and stores glucose as glycogen postprandially and releases glucose during fasting, buffering blood levels. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 9"},{"q":"Glycolysis in the cytoplasm yields, per glucose molecule, a net of...","choices":["2 ATP and lactate, which can occur only with oxygen present","no ATP at all, serving only to store glucose as glycogen","2 ATP and 2 pyruvate, even in the absence of oxygen","36 ATP and carbon dioxide, requiring oxygen throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glycolysis produces a net of 2 ATP (and 2 NADH) and 2 pyruvate molecules per glucose, and it proceeds without oxygen; this modest yield is why anaerobic tissues rely on it but generate far less ATP than aerobic metabolism. — Ch 68, slides 10-16"},{"q":"Glucagon and insulin have opposing effects on blood glucose in that glucagon...","choices":["lowers blood glucose while insulin raises it","has no effect on blood glucose, unlike insulin","raises blood glucose while insulin lowers it","and insulin both raise blood glucose together"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon (from pancreatic alpha cells) raises blood glucose by promoting hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, whereas insulin (from beta cells) lowers it by promoting cellular uptake and storage; together they keep glucose within a narrow range. — Ch 68, slides 4, 30; Ch 71, slide 9"},{"q":"The pentose phosphate pathway is important mainly because it produces...","choices":["NADPH for biosynthesis and ribose for nucleic acids","large amounts of ATP for muscle contraction","urea, used to dispose of the body's excess nitrogen","lactate to regenerate NAD+ during exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pentose phosphate (hexose monophosphate) shunt diverts glucose-6-phosphate to generate NADPH — the reducing power for fatty-acid and other biosynthesis and for antioxidant defense — and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide and nucleic-acid synthesis, rather than making ATP. — Ch 68, slides 28-29"},{"q":"During a sudden fight-or-flight response, epinephrine helps supply energy by...","choices":["blocking the release of glucose into the blood","stimulating rapid glycogen breakdown to raise blood glucose","promoting glycogen storage to conserve glucose","converting glucose into structural protein in the body's tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine promotes glycogenolysis in the liver (and muscle), quickly liberating glucose to fuel a stress response; it also mobilizes fatty acids, providing a rapid surge of available energy substrates. — Ch 68, slide 30; Ch 71, slide 9"},{"q":"Fat is the body's most efficient long-term energy store largely because it...","choices":["yields more energy per gram and is stored with little associated water","provides the least energy per gram of any fuel under most normal conditions","is stored with large amounts of water for quick access","can be used only by the liver and not by other tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fat provides about 9 kcal per gram (versus ~4 for carbohydrate or protein) and is stored in a nearly anhydrous form, so it packs far more energy per unit weight than glycogen, which is stored with substantial water — making fat the ideal compact reserve. — Ch 72, slides 4-5; Ch 73, slide 12"},{"q":"Cells take up cholesterol-rich LDL particles by...","choices":["binding them to LDL receptors and internalizing them","allowing LDL to diffuse freely across the membrane","converting LDL into HDL at the cell surface","digesting LDL with pancreatic lipase in the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cells express LDL receptors that bind circulating LDL and bring it in by receptor-mediated endocytosis, delivering cholesterol for membrane and steroid synthesis; too few functional receptors leave LDL in the blood and raise plasma cholesterol. — Ch 69, slide 36"},{"q":"Insulin promotes fat storage in part by...","choices":["blocking lipoprotein lipase so tissues cannot take up fat","inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase so stored triglyceride is not broken down","stimulating ketone body production from the mobilized fatty acids in the liver","activating hormone-sensitive lipase to release fatty acids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Insulin suppresses hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, preventing breakdown of stored triglyceride, while it also stimulates lipoprotein lipase and fatty-acid synthesis, together favoring net fat storage in the fed state. — Ch 69, slide 22; Ch 73, slide 15"},{"q":"High-density lipoprotein (HDL) is often called 'good' cholesterol because it...","choices":["delivers cholesterol from the liver to the artery walls","carries excess cholesterol from tissues back to the liver for disposal","prevents the liver from making any cholesterol at all in the body's tissues","is the main particle that carries dietary fat from the gut"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. HDL performs reverse cholesterol transport, picking up excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and returning it to the liver for excretion in bile; this helps clear cholesterol from vessel walls, in contrast to LDL, which delivers cholesterol to tissues. — Ch 69, slides 6, 36"},{"q":"The urea cycle is the body's solution to the problem that...","choices":["amino acids contain too little nitrogen to be useful","the body cannot break down amino acids at all during this particular process in the tissues","glucose must be converted into protein for storage","ammonia from amino-acid breakdown is toxic and must be converted for safe excretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Deamination of amino acids releases ammonia, which is toxic even at low levels; the liver's urea cycle converts ammonia into urea, a far safer compound that the kidneys excrete, allowing the body to dispose of nitrogen without ammonia toxicity. — Ch 70, slides 16-17"},{"q":"The body maintains an amino-acid pool that is best described as...","choices":["a reserve used only for energy and never for building protein","a set of amino acids that cannot be exchanged or replaced","a dynamic supply drawn on for protein synthesis and replenished by turnover and diet","a fixed, permanent store of protein that is never used under most conditions in the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Free amino acids form a pool that is continuously used to build new proteins and replenished by dietary intake and by the breakdown (turnover) of existing body proteins; this balance lets the body remodel and repair proteins constantly. — Ch 70, slides 3-6"},{"q":"Growth hormone promotes a positive nitrogen balance by...","choices":["stimulating amino-acid uptake and protein synthesis in tissues","accelerating the breakdown of tissue protein for fuel","blocking all amino acids from entering the cells in the body's tissues","converting body protein directly into stored fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Growth hormone enhances the transport of amino acids into cells and stimulates protein synthesis while sparing protein from breakdown; the net anabolic effect builds tissue protein, producing a positive nitrogen balance during growth. — Ch 70, slide 19"},{"q":"Short-term meal termination is promoted by signals such as...","choices":["leptin secreted by fat cells over days to weeks","a steady fall in blood glucose occurring throughout the course of the meal","gastric distension and CCK release acting through vagal afferents","ghrelin rising steadily throughout the meal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. As the stomach fills, stretch is signaled by vagal afferents, and fat/protein in the duodenum releases CCK; together these short-term signals promote satiety and help end the meal, acting over minutes rather than days. — Ch 72, slide 11"},{"q":"During prolonged starvation, ketone bodies become important because they...","choices":["are stored in large amounts before starvation begins","provide an alternative fuel for the brain, sparing body protein","are the only fuel the body can produce from glucose","prevent the body from using any fat for energy in the body's tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As fasting continues, the brain adapts to use ketone bodies produced by the liver from fat; this reduces the brain's demand for glucose and therefore lessens the breakdown of body protein for gluconeogenesis, prolonging survival. — Ch 72, slides 17-19; Ch 69, slides 15-19"},{"q":"Destruction of the lateral hypothalamic nuclei tends to cause...","choices":["excessive eating, since this is the satiety center","essentially no change in appetite or feeding behavior whatsoever","an inability to regulate body temperature only","reduced eating and weight loss, since this is the feeding center"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lateral hypothalamus acts as the feeding (hunger) center; lesions here remove the drive to eat, producing reduced intake and weight loss (inanition). This complements the ventromedial nuclei, which serve as the satiety center. — Ch 72, slide 9"},{"q":"In a sedentary person, the largest component of daily energy expenditure is...","choices":["the energy cost of occasional vigorous physical exercise sessions","shivering to maintain body temperature","the basal metabolic rate needed to sustain resting functions","the thermic effect of digesting food"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In most sedentary people the basal metabolic rate — the energy to run vital resting processes — accounts for the majority (roughly 50–70%) of daily expenditure, far exceeding the smaller contributions of activity and the thermic effect of food. — Ch 72, slide 2; Ch 73, slide 10"},{"q":"Leptin is best described as a signal that...","choices":["reports the size of fat stores and reduces appetite over the long term","directly digests dietary fat within the lumen of the small intestine","triggers hunger within minutes of the stomach emptying","is released mainly by the stomach in order to help initiate each separate meal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leptin is secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass; higher stores mean more leptin, which acts on the hypothalamus to reduce appetite and increase expenditure, providing long-term regulation of body weight. — Ch 72, slides 13-15"},{"q":"Indirect calorimetry estimates metabolic rate by measuring...","choices":["the carbon dioxide dissolved in the arterial blood under these conditions","the total nitrogen excreted in the urine over a day","the heat radiated directly from the whole body surface over time","oxygen consumption, since most energy release depends on oxygen use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because about 95% of the body's energy release comes from reactions consuming oxygen, oxygen consumption is a reliable proxy for metabolic rate; indirect calorimetry measures O2 use rather than heat directly. — Ch 73, slide 7"},{"q":"Sympathetic activation can raise metabolic rate in part by...","choices":["blocking thyroid hormone from acting on tissues","lowering the core body temperature in order to reduce metabolic demand","stimulating heat production, including thermogenesis in brown fat","shutting down all cellular metabolism to save energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic activity increases cellular metabolism and can drive thermogenesis, notably in brown adipose tissue, raising heat production and metabolic rate; this contributes to the metabolic response to cold and stress. — Ch 73, slide 9"},{"q":"Larger individuals tend to have a higher total basal metabolic rate partly because...","choices":["a bigger body always has a much lower body temperature","surface area has no relationship to heat loss or metabolism","larger bodies have proportionally less active tissue during this process","a greater body mass and surface area require more energy to maintain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Basal metabolic rate scales with body size: more metabolically active tissue and a larger surface area (over which heat is lost) mean more total energy is needed to maintain the resting body, which is why BMR is often expressed relative to body size. — Ch 73, slides 8-11"},{"q":"One reason men tend to have a higher basal metabolic rate than women is...","choices":["a smaller total body surface area losing less heat","a higher ratio of metabolically active lean muscle to fat","the absence of any thermic effect from their food","a somewhat lower body temperature that tends to speed up reactions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lean muscle is more metabolically active than fat, and men on average carry a higher muscle-to-fat ratio (linked to testosterone), which raises their resting energy expenditure relative to women. — Ch 73, slides 9, 11"},{"q":"Physical activity raises total energy expenditure primarily because...","choices":["the basal metabolic rate itself falls during exercise","body temperature drops, forcing more heat production during this process in the tissues","digestion of food stops so all energy goes to movement","contracting muscles greatly increase their consumption of energy substrates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exercise is the most variable component of energy expenditure: working muscles sharply increase their use of glucose and fatty acids to fuel contraction, raising oxygen consumption and total metabolic rate well above the resting level. — Ch 73, slides 10-12"},{"q":"On exposure to cold, the posterior hypothalamus conserves and generates heat by...","choices":["driving cutaneous vasoconstriction and shivering to raise heat production","lowering the overall metabolic rate in order to reduce the body's oxygen demand","inhibiting thyroxine release to slow chemical thermogenesis","stimulating sweating and cutaneous vasodilation to trap and hold body warmth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cold responses include sympathetic cutaneous vasoconstriction and shivering, which conserve and generate heat. — Ch 74, slides 14-15"},{"q":"Heat loss by conduction and convection differ in that convection...","choices":["requires evaporation of sweat to remove any heat","carries heat away by the movement of air or fluid over the skin","emits heat as infrared waves without any medium in this scenario","transfers heat only to objects touching the skin directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Conduction transfers heat to objects or air in direct contact, while convection removes heat as moving air or water carries the warmed layer away and replaces it with cooler medium; a breeze increases convective loss. — Ch 74, slides 6-9"},{"q":"With repeated exposure to a hot environment, acclimatization improves heat tolerance largely by...","choices":["shutting down cutaneous blood flow to the skin as a general rule","reducing the sweating rate to conserve body water","lowering the hypothalamic set point permanently","increasing the sweating rate and conserving salt in the sweat"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Heat acclimatization enhances the capacity to sweat earlier and more profusely (improving evaporative cooling) and, via aldosterone, conserves sodium in the sweat, protecting the body's salt balance during heavy sweating. — Ch 74, slides 8-10"},{"q":"Which is a genuine mechanism of body heat loss?","choices":["Vasoconstriction, the narrowing of skin vessels","Radiation, the emission of infrared heat waves to cooler surroundings","Shivering, the production of heat by muscle contraction","Piloerection, the raising of the body hairs to shed heat to the surroundings"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Radiation transfers heat as infrared waves from the warm body to cooler surrounding objects and is a major route of heat loss at rest; the other options listed are heat-conserving or heat-generating responses, not means of losing heat. — Ch 74, slides 6, 13-14"},{"q":"When surrounding air is cooler than the skin, the body's largest avenue of heat loss at rest is...","choices":["warming of inhaled air within the respiratory passages","evaporation of sweat driven by sympathetic cholinergic fibers","radiation of infrared heat waves to cooler nearby objects","conduction of heat directly into the solid objects being touched"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: at rest in cool surroundings, radiation of infrared waves is the dominant route of heat loss. — Ch 74, slide 6"}]};
